/ef/efcfb7a56c85727ded8a9d5c9bd75fd79ac0bc20ba202c2da6aaca3c4e3ba503.svg)\n\nSo, in the Cartesian equation, we remove the ![](images/74/7435bec0e47e7cfafdcbdbf0763b77d55a16344504663512cbbcf8b75a50381f.svg) part and instead write ![](images/6b/6be447d259b9b63c2cdd7adc4107d7c96ed4df1730890fc1bb5ce7cd585b8ea6.svg) as follows:\n\n![](images/92/92f849bc9c2fc37e54e4ecf4901b06624536d835db97d31f94ab77573d6e401b.svg)" 13194139534178 17592186045417 true] [17592186119656 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation for the line with the following vector equation:\n\n![](images/4b/4b3aa22ba1342c45d422c227258837ae080ee705f2580437c775bd2beaf8afeb.png)" 13194139534178 17592186045417 false] [17592186119656 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation for the line with the following vector equation:\n\n![](images/5c/5cb83f5140e0f687997fea631f78bb9d5cf7d6cbeef52fe746f4abcba9230a1a.svg)" 13194139534178 17592186045417 true] [17592186119656 1074 "Using the usual formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}{4}=\\dfrac{y-2}{0}=\\dfrac{z-3}{5} = t,\t\\qquad t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}{4}=\\dfrac{y-2}{0}=\\dfrac{z-3}{5},\n$$\n\nwhich is problematic because $\\dfrac{y-2}{0}$ involves division by zero.\n\nHowever, notice that in our line, the $y$ component always maintains the constant value of $y=2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/19/1998dafc905e62617ac4c8f57285a0d75cf0ef8bddf1e59be7eeea9149485017.svg)\n\nSo, in the Cartesian equation, we remove the $\\dfrac{y-2}{0}$ part and instead write $y=2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}{4}=\\dfrac{z-3}{5}, \\; y=2\n$$" 13194139534178 17592186045417 true] [17592186119656 1074 "Using the usual formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}{4}=\\dfrac{y-2}{0}=\\dfrac{z-3}{5} = t,\t\\qquad t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}{4}=\\dfrac{y-2}{0}=\\dfrac{z-3}{5},\n$$\n\nwhich is problematic because $\\dfrac{y-2}{0}$ involves division by zero.\n\nHowever, notice that in our line, the $y$ component always maintains the constant value of $y=2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/ca/cafe7ac2b6224a50a288cabf6b6838c6a85a722404c9ebd20238f689f196b841.png)\n\nSo, in the Cartesian equation, we remove the $\\dfrac{y-2}{0}$ part and instead write $y=2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}{4}=\\dfrac{z-3}{5}, \\; y=2\n$$" 13194139534178 17592186045417 false] [17592186119826 1038 #edb/ref 17592186413265 13194139534178 17592186045417 true] [17592186119826 1038 #edb/ref 17592186413268 13194139534178 17592186045417 true] [17592186119826 1038 #edb/ref 17592186413269 13194139534178 17592186045417 true] [17592186119826 1038 #edb/ref 17592186413273 13194139534178 17592186045417 true] [17592186119827 1038 #edb/ref 17592186413270 13194139534178 17592186045417 true] [17592186119828 1038 #edb/ref 17592186413267 13194139534178 17592186045417 true] [17592186119842 1038 #edb/ref 17592186413266 13194139534178 17592186045417 true] [17592186119842 1038 #edb/ref 17592186413271 13194139534178 17592186045417 true] [17592186119842 1038 #edb/ref 17592186413272 13194139534178 17592186045417 true] [17592186120015 1050 420.0 13194139534178 17592186045417 true] [17592186120221 1068 #edb/ref 17592186117487 13194139534178 17592186045417 false] [17592186120221 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413258 13194139534178 17592186045417 true] [17592186120221 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534178 17592186045417 true] [17592186120221 1071 "Consider the straight line with the vector equation\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Find the Cartesian equation of this line." 13194139534178 17592186045417 true] [17592186120221 1071 "Consider the straight line with the vector equation\n$r = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} + t\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nfor $t ∈ (- ∞, ∞)$. Find the Cartesian equation of this line." 13194139534178 17592186045417 false] [17592186120221 1074 "We have the vector equation \n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis implies that the straight line passes through the point $P(0,-5,5)$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨6,1,-6⟩.$ So, its Cartesian (canonical) equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-0}{6}=\\frac{y-(-5)}{1}=\\frac{z-5}{-6}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSimplifying, we reach\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{6}=\\frac{y+5}{1}=\\frac{z-5}{-6}.\n$$" 13194139534178 17592186045417 true] [17592186121048 1064 "Suppose we know that the vector equation of the line $l$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand we want to rewrite it using Cartesian coordinates. In other words, we want to create an equation that relates $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ to each other.\n\nWe can do this using the following $3$ steps.\n\n**Step 1:** Find the parametric equations for the line.\n\nSubstituting $[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]$ into the equation of $l,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3+6t \\\\ -2+5t \\\\ 5-t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=3+6t \\\\ y=-2+5t \\\\ z=5-t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Solve for $t$ in each of the parametric equations.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=3+6t \\\\ y=-2+5t \\\\ z=5-t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Equate the resulting expressions. Because the right-hand side of each equation is equal to $t,$ we can write this system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-3}{6}=\\dfrac{y+2}{5}=\\dfrac{z-5}{-1} = t\n$$\n\nWe can simplify further by omitting the \"$=t$\" on the right-hand side, which gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-3}{6}=\\dfrac{y+2}{5}=\\dfrac{z-5}{-1} \\,.\n$$\n\nAnd we are done! \n\nThis way of writing the equation of a line is called the **Cartesian equation** or **Cartesian canonical equation** (or simply **canonical equation**).\n\nIn general, the Cartesian equation of the straight line that passes through the point $P(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ and is parallel to the vector $\\mathbf{v}=\\langle v_x, v_y, v_z \\rangle$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-x_0}{v_x}=\\dfrac{y-y_0}{v_y}=\\dfrac{z-z_0}{v_z} = t, \\qquad t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$" 13194139534178 17592186045417 false] [17592186121048 1064 "Suppose we know that the vector equation of the line $l$ is given by\n![](images/92/92c5ed55727717320b9c128adf1a6d786666adb38cd6ba71d57917e60d661fd8.svg)\n\nand we want to rewrite it using Cartesian coordinates. In other words, we want to create an equation that relates $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ to each other.\n\nWe can do this using the following $3$ steps.\n\n**Step 1:** Find the parametric equations for the line.\n\nSubstituting ![](images/67/67b8360f90540ffb75d39717c375f0ef6e9a8a39cae5832c78957105e0d148b6.svg) into the equation of $l,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3+6t \\\\ -2+5t \\\\ 5-t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=3+6t \\\\ y=-2+5t \\\\ z=5-t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Solve for $t$ in each of the parametric equations.\n\n![](images/ee/eed0a210b121af73bc2ceab04b95def59239a742055284e706a1532b47edc60d.svg)\n\n**Step 3:** Equate the resulting expressions. Because the right-hand side of each equation is equal to $t,$ we can write this system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-3}{6}=\\dfrac{y+2}{5}=\\dfrac{z-5}{-1} = t\n$$\n\nWe can simplify further by omitting the \"$=t$\" on the right-hand side, which gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-3}{6}=\\dfrac{y+2}{5}=\\dfrac{z-5}{-1} \\,.\n$$\n\nAnd we are done! \n\nThis way of writing the equation of a line is called the **Cartesian equation** or **Cartesian canonical equation** (or simply **canonical equation**).\n\nIn general, the Cartesian equation of the straight line that passes through the point $P(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ and is parallel to the vector $\\mathbf{v}=\\langle v_x, v_y, v_z \\rangle$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-x_0}{v_x}=\\dfrac{y-y_0}{v_y}=\\dfrac{z-z_0}{v_z} = t, \\qquad t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).\n$$" 13194139534178 17592186045417 true] [17592186121055 1064 "Suppose we want to write the Cartesian equation for the line with the following vector equation:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nUsing the usual formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}{4}=\\dfrac{y-2}{5}=\\dfrac{z-3}{0} = t,\t\\qquad t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty),\n$$\n\nor equivalently,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}{4}=\\dfrac{y-2}{5}=\\dfrac{z-3}{0}.\n$$\n\nBut the above equation is problematic because $\\dfrac{z-3}{0}$ involves division by zero.\n\nHowever, notice that in our line, the $z$ component always maintains the constant value of $z=3\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1+4t \\\\ 2+5t \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIf we solve for $t$ in the parametric equations of the line, we get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=1+4t \\\\ y=2+5t \\\\ z=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOnly the first two equations have right-hand sides equal to $t,$ so we combine them but keep the third equation separate:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}{4}=\\dfrac{y-2}{5}, \\; z=3\n$$\n\nSo, it's as if we removed the $\\dfrac{z-3}{0}$ part in the original formula and replaced it with $z=3.$" 13194139534178 17592186045417 false] [17592186121055 1064 "Suppose we want to write the Cartesian equation for the line with the following vector equation:\n![](images/9d/9d4a6d56170de2e0dbfa7e2a6f51905b19f9ada9fa1900aafe2672f54831be86.svg)\n\nUsing the usual formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}{4}=\\dfrac{y-2}{5}=\\dfrac{z-3}{0} = t,\t\\qquad t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty),\n$$\n\nor equivalently,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}{4}=\\dfrac{y-2}{5}=\