n (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534178 17592186045417 true] [17592186413266 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413255 13194139534178 17592186045417 true] [17592186413266 1069 "q-71037" 13194139534178 17592186045417 true] [17592186413266 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534178 17592186045417 true] [17592186413266 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation for the line with the following vector equation:\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$" 13194139534178 17592186045417 true] [17592186413266 1072 #uuid "2f4d4cbd-f2d7-5e35-a92b-2f2fb49fe824" 13194139534178 17592186045417 true] [17592186413266 1074 "Using the usual formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-0}{4}=\\frac{y-(-3)}{0}=\\frac{z-4}{1}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{4}=\\frac{y+3}{0}=z-4,\n$$\n\nwhich is problematic because $\\frac{y+3}{0}$ involves division by zero.\n\nHowever, notice that in our line, the $y$ component always maintains the constant value of $y=-3:$\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}4t \\\\ -3 \\\\ 4+t\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\nSo, in the Cartesian equation, we remove the $\\frac{y+3}{0}$ part and instead write $y=-3,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{4}=z-4,\\,y=-3\n$$" 13194139534178 17592186045417 true] [17592186413267 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413261 13194139534178 17592186045417 true] [17592186413267 1069 "q-127464" 13194139534178 17592186045417 true] [17592186413267 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534178 17592186045417 true] [17592186413267 1071 "Find a Cartesian equation of the straight line that passes through $P(7,-1,0)$ and is parallel to $v=⟨-3,1,-4⟩.$" 13194139534178 17592186045417 true] [17592186413267 1072 #uuid "999b5494-410f-5564-a5e2-d9dbe0ae34c9" 13194139534178 17592186045417 true] [17592186413267 1074 "Remember that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the straight line that passes through the point $P({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$, is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-7}{-3}=\\frac{y-(-1)}{1}=\\frac{z-0}{-4}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\nor, equivalently,\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-7}{-3}=\\frac{y+1}{1}=\\frac{z}{-4}.\n$$" 13194139534178 17592186045417 true] [17592186413268 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413256 13194139534178 17592186045417 true] [17592186413268 1069 "q-127076" 13194139534178 17592186045417 true] [17592186413268 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534178 17592186045417 true] [17592186413268 1071 "Find the parametric equations of the line $\\frac{x-10}{-6}=\\frac{y+5}{3}=\\frac{z+7}{2}.$" 13194139534178 17592186045417 true] [17592186413268 1072 #uuid "bc81db7e-a314-5809-b5c5-608d6bc1d824" 13194139534178 17592186045417 true] [17592186413268 1074 "Remember that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the straight line that passes through the point $P({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$, is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nComparing the above to our given equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-10}{-6}=\\frac{y-(-5)}{3}=\\frac{z-(-7)}{2},\n$$\n\nwe find that our line passes through the point $P(10,-5,-7)$ and is parallel to $v=⟨-6,3,2⟩.$ Therefore, its vector equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations are the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=10-6t \\\\ y=-5+3t \\\\ z=-7+2t\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534178 17592186045417 true] [17592186413269 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413264 13194139534178 17592186045417 true] [17592186413269 1069 "q-127074" 13194139534178 17592186045417 true] [17592186413269 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534178 17592186045417 true] [17592186413269 1071 "Find the parametric equations of the line $\\frac{x}{7}=\\frac{y-3}{2}=\\frac{z+8}{-6}.$" 13194139534178 17592186045417 true] [17592186413269 1072 #uuid "ca426087-2ced-544a-8fc8-80a74f2884ad" 13194139534178 17592186045417 true] [17592186413269 1074 "Remember that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the straight line that passes through the point $P({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$, is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nComparing the above to our given equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{7}=\\frac{y-3}{2}=\\frac{z-(-8)}{-6},\n$$\n\nwe find that our line passes through the point $P(0,3,-8)$ and is parallel to $v=⟨7,2,-6⟩.$ Therefore, its vector equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations are the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=7t \\\\ y=3+2t \\\\ z=-8-6t\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534178 17592186045417 true] [17592186413270 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413259 13194139534178 17592186045417 true] [17592186413270 1069 "q-33246" 13194139534178 17592186045417 true] [17592186413270 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534178 17592186045417 true] [17592186413270 1071 "Consider the straight line with the parametric equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=3-4t \\\\ y=5+2t \\\\ z=-3+6t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nfor $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Find the Cartesian equation of this line." 13194139534178 17592186045417 true] [17592186413270 1072 #uuid "cf27c214-ec1e-55e8-948b-06e561f6f7e7" 13194139534178 17592186045417 true] [17592186413270 1074 "First, let's find the corresponding vector equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3-4t \\\\ 5+2t \\\\ -3+6t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-4t \\\\ 2t \\\\ 6t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nThis implies that the straight line passes through the point $P(3,5,-3)$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨-4,2,6⟩.$ So, its Cartesian (canonical) equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-3}{-4}=\\frac{y-5}{2}=\\frac{z-(-3)}{6}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSimplifying, we reach\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-3}{-4}=\\frac{y-5}{2}=\\frac{z+3}{6}.\n$$" 13194139534178 17592186045417 true] [17592186413271 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413260 13194139534178 17592186045417 true] [17592186413271 1069 "q-127459" 13194139534178 17592186045417 true] [17592186413271 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534178 17592186045417 true] [17592186413271 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation for the line with the following vector equation:\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$" 13194139534178 17592186045417 true] [17592186413271 1072 #uuid "dbddc051-609a-50f8-b7fd-7c6e4c6d692b" 13194139534178 17592186045417 true] [17592186413271 1074 "Using the usual formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-7}{0}=\\frac{y-(-2)}{1}=\\frac{z-0}{-5}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-7}{0}=\\frac{y+2}{1}=\\frac{z}{-5},\n$$\n\nwhich is problematic because $\\frac{x-7}{0}$ involves division by zero.\n\nHowever, notice that in our line, the $x$ component always maintains the constant value of $x=7:$\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -2+t \\\\ -5t\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\nSo, in the Cartesian equation, we remove the $\\frac{x-7}{0}$ part and instead write $x=7,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{y+2}{1}=\\frac{z}{-5},\\,x=7\n$$" 13194139534178 17592186045417 true] [17592186413272 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413262 13194139534178 17592186045417 true] [17592186413272 1069 "q-127460" 13194139534178 17592186045417 true] [17592186413272 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534178 17592186045417 true] [17592186413272 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation for the line with the following vector equation:\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 13\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$" 13194139534178 17592186045417 true] [17592186413272 1072 #uuid "e1761a52-f68d-5dfd-83a7-02fd5760a09e" 13194139534178 17592186045417 true] [17592186413272 1074 "Using the usual formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-1}{-2}=\\frac{y-(-6)}{5}=\\frac{z-13}{0}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-1}{-2}=\\frac{y+6}{5}=\\frac{z-13}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is problematic because $\\frac{z-13}{0}$ involves division by zero.\n\nHowever, notice that in our line, the $z$ component always maintains the constant value of $z=13:$\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}1-2t \\\\ -6+5t \\\\ 13\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\nSo, in the Cartesian equation, we remove the $\\frac{z-13}{0}$ part and instead write $z=13$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-1}{-2}=\\frac{y+6}{5},\\,z=13\n$$" 13194139534178 17592186045417 true] [17592186413273 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413257 13194139534178 17592186045417 true] [17592186413273 1069 "q-127073" 13194139534178 17592186045417 true] [17592186413273 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534178 17592186045417 true] [17592186413273 1071 "Find the parametric equations of the line $\\frac{x-13}{-7}=\\frac{y+2}{3}=\\frac{z-1}{5}.$" 13194139534178 17592186045417 true] [17592186413273 1072 #uuid "e2f7b0c6-34b9-5cdd-a2c3-dccbfbacb488" 13194139534178 17592186045417 true] [17592186413273 1074 "Remember that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the straight line that passes through the point $P({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$, is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nComparing the above to our given equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-13}{-7}=\\frac{y-(-2)}{3}=\\frac{z-1}{5},\n$$\n\nwe find that our line passes through the point $P(13,-2,1)$ and is parallel to $v=⟨-7,3,5⟩.$ Therefore, its vector equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations are the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=13-7t \\\\ y=-2+3t \\\\ z=1+5t\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534178 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-03a0481f-9850-51a2-93a8-9cb1375be70e" 17592186413205 "answer-089f0314-d541-57da-8474-803ac5e9d9f4" 17592186413206 "answer-0ac0ca21-0772-5ea6-97f1-1ae436ee1c38" 17592186413207 "answer-13aa6270-4f47-57d9-96d5-e04d9559c1dd" 17592186413208 "answer-1c2d99a9-ddfb-5a97-abd4-e05bcb1d544a" 17592186413209 "answer-299601c1-c189-56c9-a263-8a4e23810703" 17592186413210 "answer-2aa0077e-181b-5c87-b397-bf90c7b66f48" 17592186413211 "answer-2ff9e3e1-5e91-5965-a734-3ac520d19c3e" 17592186413212 "answer-38f146eb-7159-527b-9b0e-80360837fbdb" 17592186413213 "answer-3a1b04c5-6fb6-5d48-9648-cb591bb11169" 17592186413214 "answer-44b3c7a8-f3fd-53ad-a5d7-8900d29452ba" 17592186413215 "answer-4aeef7c0-439a-5ccb-8d85-ceb73b3ed2da" 17592186413216 "answer-4c141cec-318c-5fa6-984e-d5d96ba7c25e" 17592186413217 "answer-4c21e0bb-ee8c-5fa2-ad4c-8c70768e7b7f" 17592186413218 "answer-5147f586-12cc-5846-9c2a-91a6e6aae45a" 17592186413219 "answer-55d1767d-6142-57fd-84ac-0823297abb7b" 17592186413220 "ans